{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785092231,"nonce":"Imw0TNvtDmDjnRHmhAIwNQD71ag=","title":"ADR-0061 s2b: CSI ephemeral inline volume + FUSE lazy lower layer","message":"The node driver — ADR-0061 part 2, the load-bearing part. s0 measured the\n`exec` tar tunnel at 4–15% of a Step boundary and the CAS legs at 81–88%,\nand finding 2 says the CAS cost tracks FILE COUNT. Lazy materialisation is\nthe only mechanism that attacks file count without authors declaring\nanything. s3-feed removes the tunnel; **this** slice is where the win is.\n\nExplicitly NOT a one-session slice: a CSI node plugin + a FUSE filesystem +\nan overlay mount + an image + a DaemonSet + a live cluster run. Claiming it\nin one session would be the next \"reports success but structurally cannot\nwork\" finding in this repo.\n\nDepends on: s1 (workspace service + `ContentSource`), s3-feed.\nBlocks: s3-drain, s6 (66fc0e3).\n\n---\n\n\nhostPath loses on ADR-0039: it puts a `hostPath` volume in **every step\nPod**, which the hardened restricted baseline is precisely about (ADR-0039\nkeeps `host-path`/`host-network` out of the grant vocabulary deliberately),\nand PSA `restricted` forbids it. It also needs `mountPropagation:\nHostToContainer` on the step's mount plus a marker-file race —\nreintroducing the doorstop pattern ADR-0061 part 3 deletes. CSI keeps the\nstep Pod clean, makes unmount the kubelet's problem, and `tokenRequests`\ngives the driver a projected SA token, a better auth story than anything\nhostPath reaches.\n\n**Write this where a reviewer will look:** ADR-0042 explicitly rejected node\nagents (\"reintroduces a node agent\"). ADR-0061 overrides that. Otherwise the\nobjection gets re-raised in review.\n\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1\nkind: CSIDriver\nmetadata: { name: workspace.scarab.io }\nspec:\n  attachRequired: false\n  podInfoOnMount: true\n  fsGroupPolicy: None          # see \"ownership contract\" below — the driver owns ownership\n  volumeLifecycleModes: [\"Ephemeral\"]\n  tokenRequests: [{ audience: scarab-workspace, expirationSeconds: 3600 }]\n  requiresRepublish: false\n```\n\n```yaml\nspec.template.spec:\n  serviceAccountName: scarab-node-driver     # NO RoleBinding: podInfoOnMount means no API reads\n  hostPID: false\n  priorityClassName: system-node-critical\n  tolerations: [{ operator: Exists }]        # MUST land on ADR-0055 baseline-tainted nodes too\n  containers:\n    - name: driver\n      image: ghcr.io/thulasi-ram/scarab-node-driver:{{ .Values.nodeDriver.tag }}\n      securityContext: { privileged: true }\n      env: [{ name: SCARAB_WORKSPACE_URL, valueFrom: { configMapKeyRef: ... } }]\n      volumeMounts:\n        - { name: plugin-dir,  mountPath: /csi }\n        - { name: pods-dir,    mountPath: /var/lib/kubelet/pods, mountPropagation: Bidirectional }\n        - { name: cache-dir,   mountPath: /var/lib/scarab/cache }\n        - { name: fuse,        mountPath: /dev/fuse }\n    - name: node-driver-registrar\n      image: registry.k8s.io/sig-storage/csi-node-driver-registrar:v2.13.0\n      args: [\"--csi-address=/csi/csi.sock\",\n             \"--kubelet-registration-path=/var/lib/kubelet/plugins/workspace.scarab.io/csi.sock\"]\n      volumeMounts: [{ name: plugin-dir, mountPath: /csi },\n                     { name: registration-dir, mountPath: /registration }]\n  volumes:\n    - { name: plugin-dir,       hostPath: { path: /var/lib/kubelet/plugins/workspace.scarab.io, type: DirectoryOrCreate } }\n    - { name: registration-dir, hostPath: { path: /var/lib/kubelet/plugins_registry, type: Directory } }\n    - { name: pods-dir,         hostPath: { path: /var/lib/kubelet/pods, type: Directory } }\n    - { name: cache-dir,        hostPath: { path: /var/lib/scarab/cache, type: DirectoryOrCreate } }\n    - { name: fuse,             hostPath: { path: /dev/fuse, type: CharDevice } }\n```\n\n\n`GetPluginInfo`, `GetPluginCapabilities`, `Probe`, `NodeGetInfo`,\n`NodeGetCapabilities`, `NodePublishVolume`, `NodeUnpublishVolume`.\n`tonic` + `tonic-build` over `csi.proto` v1.9.\n\n\nReplace the workspace `emptyDir` (`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs`,\nthe `WORKSPACE_VOLUME` arm of `build_pod`):\n\n```rust\nVolume {\n    name: WORKSPACE_VOLUME.to_string(),\n    csi: Some(k8s_openapi::api::core::v1::CSIVolumeSource {\n        driver: \"workspace.scarab.io\".to_string(),\n        read_only: Some(false),\n        volume_attributes: Some(std::collections::BTreeMap::from([\n            (\"inputs\".to_string(), spec.workspace_inputs.join(\",\")),\n        ])),\n        node_publish_secret_ref: None,\n    }),\n    ..Default::default()\n}\n```\n\nThe service URL comes from the driver's OWN env, **not** from\n`volume_attributes` — pod-spec attributes are world-readable to anyone with\n`get pod`.\n\nIn the same commit: delete `scarab-workspace-init`, its `init-done` marker,\nand `docker/wsfetch/` (s3-feed's stepping stone — its deletion is that\nslice's acceptance criterion). Keep `CTL_VOLUME` and the egress container\nuntil s3-drain lands.\n\n**No eager fallback inside the driver.** If FUSE is unavailable,\n`NodePublishVolume` returns `FAILED_PRECONDITION` naming `/dev/fuse` and the\nDaemonSet. A Step that cannot mount fails; it does not silently materialise\n4 GB. A runtime fallback is two mental models in one deployment and the fast\npath's bugs stay deferrable forever.\n\nThe moment this lands, `deploy/local-helm` sets `nodeDriver.enabled=true`\nand `just local-helm` **fails** if the DaemonSet is not Ready — the dogfood\nmust not quietly run the eager path.\n\n\n`just check` is `cargo check --workspace` on darwin; CI is `cargo nextest\nrun --workspace` on ubuntu-latest. A `cfg(target_os = \"linux\")` dep would\nmake darwin compile an empty shell and report green — **a crate compiled\n*out* is not checked, it is skipped, and a skipped member reads as\ncovered.** That is a coverage facade of exactly the kind this repo has been\nbitten by. Split instead:\n\n- `crates/scarab-workspace-fs` — **workspace member, portable.** All the\n  logic: path→inode map, manifest→tree materialisation, the node blob cache,\n  range-read assembly, `getattr` synthesis. Depends on `scarab-storage`\n  (`ContentSource`) and nothing platform-specific. Unit-testable on darwin\n  against a fake `ContentSource`. **This is where the tests live.**\n- `crates/scarab-node-driver` — **excluded.** Only the thin bindings: the\n  `fuser::Filesystem` impl delegating to `scarab-workspace-fs`, the CSI gRPC\n  server, and the `mount(2)` calls.\n\nRoot `Cargo.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[workspace]\nresolver = \"2\"\nmembers = [\"crates/*\"]\nexclude = [\"crates/scarab-node-driver\"]\n```\n\n`crates/scarab-node-driver/Cargo.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"scarab-node-driver\"\nedition = \"2021\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nlicense = \"AGPL-3.0-only\"\npublish = false\n\n[workspace]                       # excluded above =\u003e it needs its own workspace root\n\n[dependencies]\nscarab-storage      = { path = \"../scarab-storage\" }\nscarab-workspace-fs = { path = \"../scarab-workspace-fs\" }\ntokio   = { version = \"1\", features = [\"rt-multi-thread\", \"macros\", \"fs\", \"sync\", \"net\", \"signal\"] }\nreqwest = { version = \"0.13\", default-features = false, features = [\"rustls\", \"json\", \"stream\"] }\ntonic   = \"0.12\"\nprost   = \"0.13\"\nlibc    = \"0.2\"\ntracing = \"0.1\"\ntracing-subscriber = { version = \"0.3\", features = [\"env-filter\", \"json\"] }\nserde = { version = \"1\", features = [\"derive\"] }\nserde_json = \"1\"\n\n[target.'cfg(target_os = \"linux\")'.dependencies]\nfuser = { version = \"0.15\", default-features = false }\n\n[build-dependencies]\ntonic-build = \"0.12\"\n```\n\n`src/main.rs` opens with:\n\n```rust\ncompile_error!(\"scarab-node-driver is Linux-only (libfuse + overlayfs). Use `just check-driver`.\");\n```\n\nIts `Cargo.lock` is a **second committed lockfile** — check `.gitignore`\ndoes not swallow it.\n\n\n```make\ncheck-driver:\n    docker run --rm -v {{justfile_directory()}}:/w -w /w rust:1-bookworm sh -c '\\\n      apt-get update -qq \u0026\u0026 apt-get install -y -qq libfuse3-dev protobuf-compiler \u0026\u0026 \\\n      cargo check --manifest-path crates/scarab-node-driver/Cargo.toml --all-targets'\n```\n\n```yaml\n  node-driver:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: sudo apt-get update \u0026\u0026 sudo apt-get install -y libfuse3-dev protobuf-compiler\n      - run: cargo check --manifest-path crates/scarab-node-driver/Cargo.toml --all-targets\n```\n\n…and add `node-driver` to **`.github/required-checks.txt`**, or `just\npr-gate` will not see it. Branch protection 403s on this repo — that file\n*is* the gate.\n\n\nSupported, and not theoretically: stargz-snapshotter runs precisely this\n(FUSE lower + overlay upper) on GKE/EKS in production. Requirements it\nimposes on our FUSE fs: valid `d_type` in `readdir` (`fuser`'s\n`ReplyDirectory::add` takes a `FileType`, so free) and **stable inode\nnumbers across the mount's life**.\n\nMount options — set all of these explicitly:\n\n```\nmount -t overlay overlay \\\n  -o lowerdir=\u003cfuse-mnt\u003e,upperdir=\u003cup\u003e,workdir=\u003cwk\u003e,index=off,metacopy=off,redirect_dir=off,xino=off \\\n  \u003ckubelet-target-path\u003e\n```\n\n- `index=off`, `xino=off` — the lower's inode numbering is synthetic.\n- `metacopy=off` — metacopy over a FUSE lower is a known source of wrong\n  results.\n- `redirect_dir=off` — it writes `trusted.overlay.*` xattrs.\n- **Do NOT set `userxattr`** — that is for rootless/unprivileged overlay and\n  this driver is privileged.\n\n`upperdir` and `workdir` MUST be on the same filesystem and MUST NOT be\ntmpfs (trusted xattrs): `/var/lib/scarab/pods/\u003cpod-uid\u003e/{upper,work}` on the\nnode disk, via the DaemonSet's `cache-dir` hostPath.\n\n`readOnlyRootFilesystem` is a non-issue for Step Pods —\n`step_security_context` does not set it. (The *server* Pod does, via the\nchart; unrelated.)\n\n\nToday `/workspace` is writable only because the control plane extracts the\ntar as uid/gid 65532 and then runs `chmod -R g+rwX` + `chmod g+s` on dirs\nover `exec` (`drive_workspace`, with a nine-line comment explaining git-bug\nb04697f), while `fsGroup: 65532` + `supplementalGroups: [65532]` put every\nStep in that group. All caps including `DAC_OVERRIDE` are dropped, so\n**group membership is the only thing making the workspace writable.**\n\nWith a CSI volume, `CSIDriver.spec.fsGroupPolicy` decides whether the\nkubelet applies fsGroup at all:\n\n- `fsGroupPolicy: File` would make the kubelet **recursively chown the\n  mount — which forces full materialisation of the lazy lower layer**,\n  destroying the entire point of this slice.\n- Therefore `fsGroupPolicy: None`, and **the driver owns the ownership\n  contract**:\n  - it creates `upperdir` as `65532:65532` mode `02775` (setgid, so files\n    the Step creates stay in group 65532 for the drain);\n  - the FUSE layer reports `uid=65532 gid=65532` and `mode | 0o070` for\n    every entry — synthesising in the filesystem exactly what\n    `chmod -R g+rwX` does over `exec` today.\n\nGet this wrong and every Step fails `Permission denied` on its first write,\nreproducing git-bug b04697f silently. This is precisely the \"reports success\nbut structurally cannot work\" class; it needs a **live test**, not a code\nreview.\n\n\n`fuser` drives one session on one thread. A build reading tens of thousands\nof small files is exactly the workload that exposes that.\n`fuse-backend-rs` (Cloud Hypervisor / nydus) supports multi-threaded FUSE\nand is the **migration target** if throughput measures badly. Pick `fuser`\nfor the first cut *and record the risk* — do not silently ship the slow one\nand call laziness proven.\n\nAlternatives already rejected: block-level (`nbd`, `dm-thin`) addresses the\nwrong grain; a pre-populated overlay `lowerdir` is eager by definition;\n`composefs`/`erofs` is the elegant long-term shape but every production lazy\nstack (stargz-snapshotter, nydus) implements it *with* FUSE anyway.\n\n\nEKS AL2023 is 6.1; colima's VM is 6.x; a kind \"node\" is a container sharing\nthe **host** kernel, i.e. colima's on darwin. All fine for overlay + FUSE in\nprinciple. Unverified: `/dev/fuse` availability inside a kind node container\n(kind nodes are privileged, so it *should* be present).\n`deploy/local-proc/kind.yaml` has no `extraMounts` and there is no hostPath\nanywhere in the repo today — the DaemonSet in kind is entirely new ground.\n\n\n1. The CSI driver registers; `NodePublishVolume` returns; the Step sees a\n   populated `/workspace`.\n2. overlayfs accepts a FUSE `lowerdir` — on colima's kernel, on a kind node\n   container, and on EKS AL2023. **Three separate claims, three runs.**\n3. `/dev/fuse` is present and usable inside a kind node container.\n4. The DaemonSet lands on ADR-0055 baseline-tainted nodes\n   (`tolerations: [{operator: Exists}]` is a hypothesis until a tainted node\n   runs it).\n5. **A Step can write `/workspace` under `fsGroupPolicy: None`.**\n6. Laziness happens at all — a Step reading 5% of a tree transfers ~5%.\n   Until that is measured on a cluster, ADR-0061's central claim is\n   designed, not delivered.","files":null}]}